    "q-164305": {
      "dom_id": "step-q164305",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "Given the hyperboloid of two sheets ${x}^{2}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{9}-{z}^{2}=-1,$ find the domain of $z=z(x,y).$",
      "worked_solution": "From the hyperboloid equation, we make $z$ the subject as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{9}-{z}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{9}+1 & ={z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{2} & ={x}^{2}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{9}+1 \\\\ z & =\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{9}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe square root is defined only for non-negative arguments. However, notice that for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{9}+1\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $z(x,y)$ is ${R}^{2}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
